**Design summary**

| Concern | How it's met |
|---|---|
| **Encryption at rest** | AES-256-GCM encrypts every value; the stored blob is `IV ‖ ciphertext+tag` base64-encoded. |
| **Owner isolation** | Each secret lives under a key `secret:` + base64(`[uint32 ownerLen][owner UTF-8][name UTF-8]`). `get`/`remove` look up the exact key; `list` parses every key and filters by owner. The null-byte-free, length-prefixed encoding is collision-free for arbitrary Unicode owner/name strings. |
| **Tamper detection** | AES-GCM appends a 16-byte authentication tag. Any modification of the ciphertext, IV, tag, or the base64 wrapper causes `crypto.subtle.decrypt` to throw, which is re-thrown as a plain `Error`. Copying one secret's blob over another also fails the tag check. |
| **Wrong master password** | PBKDF2 derives a different key → every GCM decrypt fails → `get` throws (or the corrupted-salt path makes `createSecretStore` reject). |
| **Persistence** | All state (salt + per-secret blobs) lives in the KV store. A fresh `createSecretStore` with the same password reads the same salt and derives the same key. |
| **No imports** | Uses only the global `crypto`, `TextEncoder`/`TextDecoder`, `btoa`/`atob`, and `DataView`. |

